// Generated by tools/build_content.py. Part 7: Practicum playbook
export default {"lead": "<p>The three past Design Practicums in your files (Fall 2020, Summer 2021, Fall 2023) all have the same three questions, worth 27, 35 and 38 points. The instructor's note on the Reef example says yours will test the same knowledge with different questions, possibly reshaped for online testing.</p>", "secs": [{"id": "p7-what-the-practicum-asks", "t": "What the practicum asks", "h": "<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Points</th><th>Task</th><th>How past versions were graded</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>27</td><td>Three short scenarios. For each, draw the entities (names only) and the single relationship, binary or recursive, with minimum and maximum cardinalities.</td><td>9 points each: correct entities 4, cardinalities 4, no extra entities or relationships 1</td></tr><tr><td>2a</td><td>10</td><td>From a sample table, list every minimal functional dependency the data suggests</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>2b</td><td>7</td><td>Name the suggested FD you would least expect to survive a logical test, and say why</td><td>Any FD that is not truly logical is accepted if the explanation is sound</td></tr><tr><td>2c</td><td>6</td><td>Name an attribute that could be dropped as redundant, and say why</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>2d</td><td>12</td><td>Given a dependency diagram, normalize to 2NF, then to 3NF</td><td>6 points per stage. Deductions from 1 point for a missed key up to 3 points for removing the wrong dependency.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>38</td><td>Draw a full ERD from a one-page narrative</td><td>Fall 2023 solution sheet: entities 15, has-a relationships 10, is-a relationships 10 (8 structure, 2 exclusive or inclusive), cardinalities 10 at half a point each. That sheet totals 45, so read these as proportions of the 38.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Standing directions on Question 3: show all minimum and maximum cardinalities; do not show attributes on entities; use an association entity where a relationship needs one; do not use anything that implies a multivalued attribute; list non-obvious assumptions. ID-dependent vs non-ID-dependent is not graded. The older review problems add one more: do not use ternary relationships.</p>"}, {"id": "p7-question-1-one-relationship-drawn-exactly", "t": "Question 1: one relationship, drawn exactly", "h": "<ol><li><strong>Count the kinds of thing.</strong> If both sides of the sentence are the same kind of thing (member and roommate, patient and co-worker, faculty and supervisor), there is one entity and the relationship is recursive. Otherwise there are two.</li><li><strong>Write two sentences</strong>, one in each direction: \"Each A has [min] to [max] B.\" \"Each B has [min] to [max] A.\"</li><li><strong>Place the symbols.</strong> Sentence one goes at the B end. Sentence two goes at the A end.</li><li><strong>Add nothing else.</strong> No attributes, no third entity, no second relationship. Extra structure costs a point.</li><li><strong>Write down any assumption</strong> you made beyond the text.</li></ol><p>The past scenarios and their keyed answers:</p><div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Scenario</th><th>Each direction</th><th>Answer</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Students and sections (the example on every practicum)</td><td>A student takes zero to many sections. A section has zero to many students.</td><td><span class=\"crow\" data-a=\"Students\" data-ae=\"0,M\" data-be=\"0,M\" data-b=\"Sections\"></span></td></tr><tr><td>Wasp species and host species</td><td>A wasp species has one or more hosts. A host species has one or more wasps.</td><td><span class=\"crow\" data-a=\"Wasp_Species\" data-ae=\"1,M\" data-be=\"1,M\" data-b=\"Host_Species\"></span></td></tr><tr><td>Appearances and cities</td><td>A city has zero to many appearances. An appearance is in one and only one city.</td><td><span class=\"crow\" data-a=\"Appearances\" data-ae=\"0,M\" data-be=\"1,1\" data-b=\"City\"></span></td></tr><tr><td>Shipments and customers</td><td>A shipment has exactly one customer. A customer has zero to many shipments.</td><td><span class=\"crow\" data-a=\"Customer\" data-ae=\"1,1\" data-be=\"0,M\" data-b=\"Shipment\"></span></td></tr><tr><td>Recipes and ingredients</td><td>A recipe has one or more ingredients. An ingredient is in zero to many recipes.</td><td><span class=\"crow\" data-a=\"Recipe\" data-ae=\"0,M\" data-be=\"1,M\" data-b=\"Ingredient\"></span></td></tr><tr><td>Nurse as primary administrator of a nursing station</td><td>A nurse administers zero or one station. A station has one and only one administrator.</td><td><span class=\"crow\" data-a=\"Nurse\" data-ae=\"1,1\" data-be=\"0,1\" data-b=\"Nursing_Station\"></span></td></tr><tr><td>Infected patients who are co-workers</td><td>A patient lists zero to many patients. A patient is listed by zero to many patients.</td><td>Recursive on Infected_Patients, zero or many at both ends</td></tr><tr><td>Club members who are roommates, or who are married</td><td>A member has zero or one roommate, and is the roommate of zero or one.</td><td>Recursive on Member, zero or one at both ends</td></tr><tr><td>Faculty and their immediate supervisor</td><td>A faculty member oversees zero to many. A faculty member is supervised by zero or one.</td><td>Recursive on Faculty: zero or many at one end, zero or one at the other. Label the ends.</td></tr><tr><td>Elephants and their parents</td><td>An elephant has zero to two parents on record. An elephant is the parent of zero to many.</td><td>Recursive on Elephants: zero or many at both ends, with \"2\" written at the Parent end. Label the ends Parent and Offspring.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Lessons from the instructor's notes on these:</p><ul><li><strong>One-to-one does happen.</strong> The nurse scenario is the model: \"many nurses are not administrators\" makes the station end zero or one.</li><li><strong>Mandatory on both sides blocks recursion.</strong> Wasps and hosts could be one Species entity, but a mandatory recursive relationship would force every species to have both a host and a wasp. Keep it binary.</li><li><strong>\"Zero\" is usually hiding in a parenthetical</strong>: \"customers are added before they receive any shipments\", \"some ingredients do not appear in any current recipe\".</li></ul>"}, {"id": "p7-question-2a-find-the-functional-dependencies-in-sample-data", "t": "Question 2a: find the functional dependencies in sample data", "h": "<p>The question asks for every <strong>minimal</strong> FD that the data <strong>suggests may</strong> exist, even ones you think are nonsense. Do not filter by logic here; that is part b. Only determinants of one or two attributes are considered.</p><ol><li><strong>Single attributes, unique columns.</strong> A column with no repeated value determines everything. Write X → ALL.</li><li><strong>Single attributes, repeating columns.</strong> For each column with repeats, look at every repeated value. If the rows that share it always agree on some other column, you have an FD. One disagreement kills it. Check both directions separately.</li><li><strong>Pairs.</strong> Skip any attribute that is already a candidate key by itself; adding to it only makes a non-minimal determinant. For each remaining pair, check whether the combination is unique across all rows. If so, write A, B → ALL.</li><li><strong>Pairs that determine less than everything</strong> (A, B → C) also count if they appear, as long as neither A nor B determines C alone.</li></ol><p>Worked on the Fall 2023 table:</p><div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>StudentID</th><th>LastName</th><th>FirstName</th><th>HomeRoom</th><th>HomeTeacher</th><th>BusID</th><th>BusDriverID</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1021</td><td>Giovani</td><td>Susan</td><td>121</td><td>Razavi</td><td>48</td><td>12</td></tr><tr><td>1059</td><td>Smith</td><td>Robert</td><td>121</td><td>Razavi</td><td>45</td><td>34</td></tr><tr><td>1078</td><td>Jones</td><td>Mary</td><td>124</td><td>Polites</td><td>45</td><td>34</td></tr><tr><td>1075</td><td>Xi</td><td>Natalie</td><td>121</td><td>Razavi</td><td>73</td><td>23</td></tr><tr><td>1115</td><td>Gupta</td><td>Mary</td><td>127</td><td>CJ</td><td>43</td><td>23</td></tr><tr><td>1034</td><td>Kroenke</td><td>Charles</td><td>133</td><td>Dragon</td><td>21</td><td>56</td></tr><tr><td>1055</td><td>Sosa</td><td>Mary</td><td>134</td><td>Shanker</td><td>46</td><td>22</td></tr><tr><td>1062</td><td>Rider</td><td>Charles</td><td>123</td><td>Kwak</td><td>42</td><td>11</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Finding</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Unique columns</td><td>StudentID → ALL. LastName → ALL.</td></tr><tr><td>Repeating columns</td><td>HomeRoom → HomeTeacher (every 121 has Razavi). HomeTeacher → HomeRoom (every Razavi has 121). BusID → BusDriverID (both 45s have 34).</td></tr><tr><td>A direction that fails</td><td>BusDriverID does not determine BusID: driver 23 appears with buses 73 and 43.</td></tr><tr><td>Unique pairs (StudentID and LastName skipped)</td><td>FirstName, HomeRoom → ALL. FirstName, HomeTeacher → ALL. FirstName, BusID → ALL. FirstName, BusDriverID → ALL. HomeRoom, BusID → ALL. HomeRoom, BusDriverID → ALL. HomeTeacher, BusID → ALL. HomeTeacher, BusDriverID → ALL.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Checked by script: this table produces exactly the 13 dependencies in the instructor's key. Open the Dependency lab to test any pair of columns yourself.</p><p>The Summer 2021 movie table teaches the minimality rule: YearReleased, AveTicketPrice → ALL is struck from the key because YearReleased → ALL already holds on its own.</p>"}, {"id": "p7-question-2b-the-logical-test", "t": "Question 2b: the logical test", "h": "<p>Pick one FD from part a that the data suggests but meaning does not support, and explain what would have to be true for it to hold.</p><ul><li>Model answer (Fall 2023): LastName → ALL is not logical. Two students with the same last name, which is very likely, would break it.</li><li>Model answer (Summer 2021): YearReleased → ALL is not logical. Every movie released in a given year would need the same genre and every other value.</li><li>Model answer (Fall 2020): DateAdded → DiscountPerc is not logical. There is no reason the date an item joined the menu would set its discount.</li></ul><p>What generally survives the logical test: surrogate keys (StudentID, Movie_ID), dependencies that come from a stated formula, and real business rules such as one teacher per homeroom. Unique pairs in a small sample are usually coincidence, but not always: the Summer 2021 key accepts Title, Genre as a logical key for movies.</p>"}, {"id": "p7-question-2c-the-redundant-attribute", "t": "Question 2c: the redundant attribute", "h": "<p>Look for a <strong>derived</strong> attribute: one that can be calculated from others. The table usually comes with a note giving the formula.</p><ul><li>Fall 2020: DiscountPrice = MenuPrice × (1 − DiscountPerc). Any one of the three can be dropped, because it can be calculated from the other two.</li><li>Summer 2021: AveTicketPrice × TicketsSold = TicketSales. Keep any two.</li><li>Fall 2023: no formula, so the answer is that no attribute can be removed without losing real content.</li></ul><p>The trap: an FD is not a derivation. BusID → BusDriverID does not let you calculate the driver from the bus, so BusDriverID is not redundant.</p>"}, {"id": "p7-question-2d-normalize-from-a-dependency-diagram", "t": "Question 2d: normalize from a dependency diagram", "h": "<ol><li><strong>Read the diagram.</strong> Underlined attributes are the primary key. An arrow starting from part of the key is the partial dependency. An arrow between two non-key attributes is the transitive dependency.</li><li><strong>2NF: remove the partial dependency, and only that.</strong> New relation = determinant plus its dependents; the determinant is its primary key. Delete the dependents from the original. Keep the determinant in the original: it is still part of the primary key and is now also a foreign key.</li><li><strong>3NF: start from your 2NF answer</strong>, not from the original table. Remove the transitive dependency the same way. Its determinant stays in the original as a foreign key.</li><li><strong>Mark everything.</strong> Solid underline for primary keys, dashed underline for foreign keys, and an arrow from each foreign key to the primary key it references.</li></ol><p>The Fall 2023 version (DeliveryVehicleAccidents) is worked in Part 5. Two more to practice on, with the keyed answers. All three are in the Normalization workshop.</p><p><strong>Summer 2021, Cities in States.</strong> Key: State, City. Partial: State → StateCapitolCity. Transitive: MayorID → MayorName.</p><ul><li>2NF: <code class=\"rel\">City(<span class=\"pk fk\">State</span>, <span class=\"pk\">City</span>, Altitude, Population, MayorID, MayorName)</code> and <code class=\"rel\">StateCapitol(<span class=\"pk\">State</span>, StateCapitolCity)</code></li><li>3NF: <code class=\"rel\">City(<span class=\"pk fk\">State</span>, <span class=\"pk\">City</span>, Altitude, Population, <span class=\"fk\">MayorID</span>)</code>, <code class=\"rel\">StateCapitol(<span class=\"pk\">State</span>, StateCapitolCity)</code> and <code class=\"rel\">CityMayor(<span class=\"pk\">MayorID</span>, MayorName)</code></li></ul><p><strong>Fall 2020, Client_Project.</strong> Key: ProjectID, ClientID. Partial: ProjectID → ProjectName. Transitive: BudgetID → TotalBudget.</p><ul><li>2NF: <code class=\"rel\">Client_Project(<span class=\"pk fk\">ProjectID</span>, <span class=\"pk\">ClientID</span>, StartDate, Duration_wk, BudgetID, TotalBudget)</code> and <code class=\"rel\">Project(<span class=\"pk\">ProjectID</span>, ProjectName)</code></li><li>3NF: <code class=\"rel\">Client_Project(<span class=\"pk fk\">ProjectID</span>, <span class=\"pk\">ClientID</span>, StartDate, Duration_wk, <span class=\"fk\">BudgetID</span>)</code>, <code class=\"rel\">Project(<span class=\"pk\">ProjectID</span>, ProjectName)</code> and <code class=\"rel\">Budget(<span class=\"pk\">BudgetID</span>, TotalBudget)</code></li></ul><p>The relation names Project and Budget are mine; the key leaves the new relations unnamed.</p>"}, {"id": "p7-question-3-the-full-erd", "t": "Question 3: the full ERD", "h": "<p>Use the five-step method from Part 3, and translate the narrative one sentence at a time.</p><div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>When the narrative says</th><th>Draw</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>\"Each X has zero to many Y; each Y belongs to one and only one X\"</td><td>Parent and child, one-to-many</td></tr><tr><td>\"Share common attributes but each has some data stored only for it\"</td><td>Supertype with subtypes</td></tr><tr><td>\"Can only be in one of the categories\", \"either but not both\"</td><td>Exclusive: X in the circle</td></tr><tr><td>\"Can serve as more than one type\"</td><td>Inclusive: empty circle</td></tr><tr><td>\"Line items\", \"statement\", \"paycheck\", \"schedule\", \"list\"</td><td>Line-item pattern: header, line item, item</td></tr><tr><td>Many-to-many, plus a date, quantity, balance or status that belongs to the pairing</td><td>Association entity between the two</td></tr><tr><td>The related things are the same kind of thing</td><td>Recursive relationship</td></tr><tr><td>\"Only [one subtype] can ...\"</td><td>A relationship attached to the subtype, not the supertype</td></tr><tr><td>\"Any number of phones and addresses\"</td><td>Separate child entities, never a multivalued attribute</td></tr><tr><td>\"Multiple years of data\", \"which list was in effect historically\"</td><td>One parent with many dated child records</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><strong>Worked problem: Fall 2023, school district pay.</strong></p><div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Narrative</th><th>Structure</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Administrators, teachers and others share most data, each has some unique data, and \"an employee can serve as more than one type\"</td><td>Employee supertype with Administrator, Teacher and Other subtypes, inclusive</td></tr><tr><td>Regular pay, overtime pay and benefits share common attributes, each has unique data, and an item \"can only be in one of the categories\"</td><td>Pay_Benefit_Item supertype with Regular, Overtime and Benefit subtypes, exclusive</td></tr><tr><td>\"Annual pay is stored in a line item model\"</td><td>Employee, Total_Pay_Benefit, Pay_Benefit_Line_Item, Pay_Benefit_Item</td></tr><tr><td>An employee has zero to many total pay records; each record has one and only one employee</td><td>Employee to Total_Pay_Benefit: one and only one, zero or many</td></tr><tr><td>A total pay record has at least one line item; each line item has exactly one record</td><td>Total_Pay_Benefit to Line_Item: one and only one, one or many</td></tr><tr><td>An item is on zero to many line items; each line item has one and only one item</td><td>Pay_Benefit_Item to Line_Item: one and only one, zero or many</td></tr><tr><td>An administrator reviews zero to many records; each record is reviewed by one and only one administrator</td><td>Administrator (the subtype) to Total_Pay_Benefit: one and only one, zero or many</td></tr><tr><td>An administrator directly administers zero to many employees; each employee has one and only one direct administrator</td><td>Administrator (the subtype) to Employee: one and only one, zero or many</td></tr></tbody></table></div><figure class=\"fig\"><div class=\"scroll\"><svg class=\"dia\" viewBox=\"0 0 760 312\" role=\"img\" preserveAspectRatio=\"xMidYMin meet\" aria-label=\"Both extra relationships attach to the Administrator subtype\" font-size=\"12\"><text x=\"24\" y=\"30\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">Both extra relationships attach to the Administrator subtype</text><g><g stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"><line x1=\"206\" y1=\"139\" x2=\"206\" y2=\"125\"></line><line x1=\"212\" y1=\"139\" x2=\"212\" y2=\"125\"></line><path d=\"M200 132L228 132\"></path></g><g stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"><path d=\"M264 125L251 132L264 139\"></path><circle cx=\"244\" cy=\"132\" r=\"5\"></circle><path d=\"M264 132L249 132M239 132L236 132\"></path></g><path d=\"M228 132H236\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"></path><g stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"><line x1=\"370\" y1=\"139\" x2=\"370\" y2=\"125\"></line><line x1=\"376\" y1=\"139\" x2=\"376\" y2=\"125\"></line><path d=\"M364 132L392 132\"></path></g><g stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"><path d=\"M428 125L415 132L428 139\"></path><line x1=\"409\" y1=\"125\" x2=\"409\" y2=\"139\"></line><path d=\"M428 132L400 132\"></path></g><path d=\"M392 132H400\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"></path><g stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"><path d=\"M538 139L551 132L538 125\"></path><circle cx=\"558\" cy=\"132\" r=\"5\"></circle><path d=\"M538 132L553 132M563 132L566 132\"></path></g><g stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"><line x1=\"596\" y1=\"125\" x2=\"596\" y2=\"139\"></line><line x1=\"590\" y1=\"125\" x2=\"590\" y2=\"139\"></line><path d=\"M602 132L574 132\"></path></g><path d=\"M566 132H574\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"></path></g><g><g stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"><path d=\"M110 125L97 132L110 139\"></path><circle cx=\"90\" cy=\"132\" r=\"5\"></circle><path d=\"M110 132L95 132M85 132L82 132\"></path></g><g stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"><line x1=\"61\" y1=\"244\" x2=\"47\" y2=\"244\"></line><line x1=\"61\" y1=\"238\" x2=\"47\" y2=\"238\"></line><path d=\"M54 250L54 222\"></path></g><path d=\"M82 132H54V222\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"></path><text x=\"62\" y=\"184\" font-size=\"11.5\" fill=\"var(--muted)\">administers</text></g><g><g stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"><path d=\"M321 110L314 97L307 110\"></path><circle cx=\"314\" cy=\"90\" r=\"5\"></circle><path d=\"M314 110L314 95M314 85L314 82\"></path></g><g stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"><line x1=\"41\" y1=\"244\" x2=\"27\" y2=\"244\"></line><line x1=\"41\" y1=\"238\" x2=\"27\" y2=\"238\"></line><path d=\"M34 250L34 222\"></path></g><path d=\"M314 82V58H34V222\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"></path><text x=\"174\" y=\"50\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11.5\" fill=\"var(--muted)\">reviews</text></g><g><rect x=\"110\" y=\"110\" width=\"90\" height=\"44\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"none\" fill-opacity=\"1\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.25\"></rect><text x=\"155\" y=\"136\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">Employee</text></g><g><rect x=\"264\" y=\"110\" width=\"100\" height=\"44\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"none\" fill-opacity=\"1\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.25\"></rect><text x=\"314\" y=\"129\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">Total_Pay</text><text x=\"314\" y=\"145\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">_Benefit</text></g><g><rect x=\"428\" y=\"110\" width=\"110\" height=\"44\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"none\" fill-opacity=\"1\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.25\"></rect><text x=\"483\" y=\"129\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">Pay_Benefit</text><text x=\"483\" y=\"145\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">_Line_Item</text></g><g><rect x=\"602\" y=\"110\" width=\"110\" height=\"44\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"none\" fill-opacity=\"1\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.25\"></rect><text x=\"657\" y=\"129\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">Pay_Benefit</text><text x=\"657\" y=\"145\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">_Item</text></g><g><path d=\"M155 154V185M155 207V226M74 250V226H238V250M165 226V250\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"></path><g><circle cx=\"155\" cy=\"196\" r=\"11\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"2\"></circle></g><rect x=\"24\" y=\"250\" width=\"100\" height=\"36\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"var(--accent)\" fill-opacity=\"0.12\" stroke=\"var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"2\"></rect><rect x=\"132\" y=\"250\" width=\"66\" height=\"36\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"none\" fill-opacity=\"1\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.25\"></rect><rect x=\"208\" y=\"250\" width=\"60\" height=\"36\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"none\" fill-opacity=\"1\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.25\"></rect><text x=\"74\" y=\"272\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">Administrator</text><text x=\"165\" y=\"272\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">Teacher</text><text x=\"238\" y=\"272\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">Other</text><text x=\"174\" y=\"200\" font-size=\"11.5\" fill=\"var(--muted)\">inclusive</text></g><g><path d=\"M657 154V185M657 207V226M547 250V226H704V250M629 226V250\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"></path><g><circle cx=\"657\" cy=\"196\" r=\"11\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"2\"></circle><path d=\"M649 188L665 204M665 188L649 204\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"2\"></path></g><rect x=\"512\" y=\"250\" width=\"70\" height=\"36\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"none\" fill-opacity=\"1\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.25\"></rect><rect x=\"591\" y=\"250\" width=\"76\" height=\"36\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"none\" fill-opacity=\"1\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.25\"></rect><rect x=\"674\" y=\"250\" width=\"60\" height=\"36\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"none\" fill-opacity=\"1\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.25\"></rect><text x=\"547\" y=\"272\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">Regular</text><text x=\"629\" y=\"272\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">Overtime</text><text x=\"704\" y=\"272\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">Benefit</text><text x=\"676\" y=\"200\" font-size=\"11.5\" fill=\"var(--muted)\">exclusive</text></g></svg></div><figcaption>Fall 2023 Question 3 solution: 10 entities, 5 has-a and 2 is-a relationships</figcaption></figure><p>Count it against the grading: 10 entities, 5 has-a relationships, 2 is-a relationships, and 20 cardinality marks (4 per has-a relationship). Two things make this problem harder than it looks. Both extra relationships attach to the Administrator subtype, because only administrators can review or administer. And Employee is related to Total_Pay_Benefit twice, once as the person paid (through Employee) and once as the reviewer (through Administrator).</p><p><strong>Fall 2020, Reef (keyed answer in words).</strong></p><div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Relationship</th><th>Parent end</th><th>Child end</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Region to Zone</td><td>One and only one</td><td>One or many</td></tr><tr><td>Zone to Site</td><td>One and only one</td><td>One or many</td></tr><tr><td>Site to Dive</td><td>One and only one</td><td>Zero or many</td></tr><tr><td>Genus to Species</td><td>One and only one</td><td>One or many</td></tr><tr><td>Species to Sighting</td><td>One and only one</td><td>Zero or many</td></tr><tr><td>Dive to Sighting</td><td>One and only one</td><td>Zero or many</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Sighting is the required association entity: species and dives are many-to-many, and dive-time, depth and environment belong to the pairing. Site is a supertype with Protected_site and Unprotected_site subtypes, shown as exclusive with full specialization. The instructor notes that exclusive vs inclusive was not stated clearly enough in that problem to be graded.</p><p><strong>Summer 2021, Rental properties (built from the problem statement; check it against the instructor's video solution).</strong> Nine entities: Rental_Property with House and Apartment subtypes (exclusive), Owner, Phone, Address, Statement, Line_Item, and Purchase.</p><ul><li>Owner to Phone, and Owner to Address: one-to-many, with at least one of each per owner. These replace multivalued attributes.</li><li>Owner and Rental_Property are many-to-many with a purchase date, so Purchase is an association entity, with one or many at its end of both lines.</li><li>Owner, Statement, Line_Item, Rental_Property is a line-item pattern: an owner has zero to many statements, a statement has one or more line items, and a property is on zero to many line items.</li></ul>"}, {"id": "p7-how-points-are-usually-lost", "t": "How points are usually lost", "h": "<ul><li>Cardinality symbols drawn at the wrong end of the line.</li><li>A missing minimum or maximum. Every end needs both.</li><li>An extra entity or relationship on Question 1.</li><li>A separate entity where the relationship should be recursive.</li><li>A many-to-many relationship left as a plain line when the narrative gives it data of its own.</li><li>Attributes drawn on ordinary entities, or the organization itself drawn as an entity.</li><li>A ternary relationship.</li><li>Subtypes drawn without choosing exclusive or inclusive.</li><li>On 2a, listing non-minimal determinants or leaving out the unique pairs.</li><li>On 2a, discarding an FD because it \"makes no sense\". That judgment belongs in 2b.</li><li>On 2d, removing the transitive dependency during the 2NF step, or starting 3NF from the original table.</li><li>On 2d, deleting the determinant from the original relation. It must stay as the foreign key.</li><li>On 2d, no dashed underlines or referential integrity arrows.</li><li>Assumptions not written down.</li></ul>"}]};
